4235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415310 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415310 1077 "selection" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415310 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415304 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415310 1079 #uuid "8daab46c-8511-5579-956f-63a995a70f85" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415284 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415292 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415294 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415295 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415303 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1077 "selection" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415294 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415311 1079 #uuid "fb1d5ecc-a424-5137-bd29-d836fb937e8e" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415307 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415312 1069 "q-125645" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415312 1071 "![](images/98/981d966645eec322afbfe28a5a1f519eeab8b3557692dcce368a87fe748dddd6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415312 1072 #uuid "2e8f010b-1d85-5f55-981d-5803f1cc03c7" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415312 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/b2/b2473eb0c505d75d2c448c1a0d714d05d42737ce8d601401e925834a25ae9e14.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/85/8513d9d15d204b6f63f310a37465d6b8dfb21239ca412bac7d3b9936f2bf5ebf.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415310 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415313 1069 "q-125470" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415313 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise." 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415313 1072 #uuid "4ec2acb3-1493-5600-bce3-9a9694ba6ec0" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415313 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415309 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415314 1069 "q-125659" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415314 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2y\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415314 1072 #uuid "77e87c43-f6db-5082-9277-93ef32960dff" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415314 1074 "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4x}{1+4{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415308 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415315 1069 "q-125481" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415315 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ oriented counterclockwise." 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415315 1072 #uuid "b79e3fb3-3865-5932-ba2e-8b5992286bc0" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true] [17592186415315 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡t+5{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534235 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-09f4c3d7-9a04-5668-b2e0-7bc01b46d0de" 17592186415282 "answer-11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" 17592186415283 "answer-152a2be2-a7fd-5859-a279-3e7945b3da34" 17592186415284 "answer-1b3484a3-4e24-56fb-b7a3-9ddea884c66d" 17592186415285 "answer-1e6bbb37-e5ee-59d8-9780-e4f18b4fd703" 17592186415286 "answer-3daf5c5a-bb1b-59ca-aa2f-2855f76bd340" 17592186415287 "answer-50325077-b1b2-55cc-a916-71cd3fe9281a" 17592186415288 "answer-587cc8ba-51a2-5f70-b7ab-e29504848366" 17592186415289 "answer-5d659d54-987d-5ce7-9597-87369b068528" 17592186415290 "answer-5f428d96-a179-52b0-a209-2ee6f08dffb0" 17592186415291 "answer-645f68e5-b7b0-5de1-8cbc-084d6b2e348a" 17592186415292 "answ